{
  "description": "This frame illustrates concepts related to spherical mirrors, primarily focusing on the geometric setup and the relationship between focal length and radius of curvature. The overall title, written in Bengali, states 'Uttol Dorpone Roshmichitro/Protibimbo' (Convex Mirror Ray Diagram/Image), although the detailed schematic below features characteristics typical of a Concave Mirror (Obtol Dorpon) analysis.\n\nThere are two main diagrams:\n1.  **Left Diagram (D1):** Shows a standard principal axis, a mirror structure (hatching suggests it's a convex mirror, reflecting surface facing left), a labeled Focus (F), and an object (arrow P standing on Q) placed along the axis.\n2.  **Right Diagram (D2):** This is a detailed schematic of a Concave Mirror (MM') setup, where the reflection occurs on the left side. The principal axis defines key geometric points: C (Center of Curvature), F (Focus), and P (Pole). Six numbered vertical arrows (1 through 6, colored red) represent different object positions relative to C, F, and P, used to analyze the six standard cases of image formation in a concave mirror.\n\nMathematical relationships are displayed below the diagram:\n*   $CP = R$ (R is the Radius of Curvature, measured from the Pole P to the Center C).\n*   $FP = f$ (f is the Focal Length, measured from the Pole P to the Focus F).\n*   A boxed formula states the fundamental relationship: $f = R/2$. \n\nThe educational purpose is to introduce the terminology (Pole, Focus, Center) and the critical mathematical relationship linking the geometric properties of spherical mirrors, using the six standard object positions as context.",
